    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "We examine the power of the test based on $ T_2 = \\widehat{p}_{22} $ first. This test statistic has mean $ p_{22} $ and variance $ p_{22}(1 - p_{22}) / n $, where $ n $ is the number of doubly affected pairs. Expressing the variance of the test statistic as $ \\sigma^2(T) = f^2 / n $, shows that here $ f^2 = p_{22}(1 - p_{22}) $. Under the null hypothesis of no linked QTL, $ p_{22} = 1/4 $, giving the mean and variance as $ \\mu_o = 1/4 $ and $ f_0^2 = (1/4)(3/4) = 3/16 $. When a linked QTL is present, so that $ p_{22} = 1/4 + d_{22} $, the mean and variance become",
